<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Gen Physiol</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">J. Gen. Physiol</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">jgenphysiol</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>The Journal of General Physiology</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1295</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-7748</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">5792362</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2225915</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Effects of Polypeptide and Protein Hormones on Lipid Monolayers </article-title><subtitle>I. <italic>Effect of insulin and parathyroid hormone on monomolecular films of monooctadecyl phosphate and stearic acid</italic></subtitle></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Kafka</surname><given-names>Marian S.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Pak</surname><given-names>Charles Y. C.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="d32e26">From the Endocrinology Branch, National Heart Institute, National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, Maryland 20014</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>7</month><year>1969</year></pub-date><volume>54</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>134</fpage><lpage>143</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>27</day><month>1</month><year>1969</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1969 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1969</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p>Insulin in low concentrations inhibits the uptake of Ca<sup>++</sup> by the monooctadecyl (stearyl) phosphate monolayer (at air-water interface) and facilitates the release of Ca<sup>++</sup> adsorbed to the monolayer. These effects of insulin are more pronounced at higher insulin concentrations. Evidence is presented that a relatively intact insulin molecule competes with Ca<sup>++</sup> for the free phosphate group of the monolayer. Albumin has a slight inhibitory action on calcium uptake and parathyroid hormone has no observable action on calcium uptake or release.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>